  "text": "To find the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere as a function of the coordinates of point \\(P(x,y,z)\\) on the surface of the cube, where the cube has an edge length of 2 centered at the origin and its vertices lie on the axes, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the coordinates of the vertices of the cube:**\n   The vertices of the cube are at \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\).\n\n2. **Determine the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere:**\n   Since the cube is positioned such that each of its vertices is on an axis, the center of the circumscribed sphere must be located at the midpoint of the line segment connecting any two opposite vertices of the cube. For example, the midpoint between \\((1,1,1)\\) and \\((-1,-1,-1)\\) is \\((0,0,0)\\), which is the origin.\n\n3. **Reasoning that the sphere is tangent to the axes:**\n   - The distance from the center of the sphere (which we found to be \\((0,0,0)\\)) to any of the axes (the x-axis, y-axis, and z-axis) is equal to the radius of the sphere.\n   - The radius of the sphere is half the diagonal length of the cube, which is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) (since the diagonal of a cube with side length 2 is \\(2\\sqrt{3}\\)).\n   - The distance from the center of the sphere \\((0,0,0)\\) to the x-axis is the maximum x-coordinate, which is 1 (or -1 if negative).\n\nTherefore, the center of the circumscribed sphere is \\((0,0,0)\\), and it is tangent to the axes at the vertices where they intersect the cube. \n\nFinal answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0,0,0)}\n\\]",
